(window.DOM_TESTS=window.DOM_TESTS||{})["circulation-2"]={"lecture":"circulation-2","chapters":["local-regulation","neural-humoral","reflexes"],"questions":[{"id":"lt-circulation-2:3ba5b788","chapter":"local-regulation","section":"1-why-does-a-tissue-need-to-control-its-own-blood-flow","type":"application","stem":"A resting muscle suddenly becomes active while systemic arterial pressure stays steady. What most directly increases its blood supply?","options":{"A":"Its local capillaries develop stronger contractions.","B":"Its arteries increase their driving pressure.","C":"Its large veins reduce their compliance.","D":"Its local arterioles reduce their resistance."},"answer":"D","why":"Flow = pressure difference ÷ resistance. With arterial pressure unchanged, the only way to get more flow is lower resistance, and the resistance that tissues control is in their arterioles. Metabolites from the working muscle relax those arterioles.","not":{"A":"Capillaries lack the smooth muscle needed for this response.","B":"The stem states that systemic arterial pressure remains steady.","C":"Vein compliance mainly affects stored volume, not the main resistance to flow."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-2:0f24d713","chapter":"local-regulation","section":"1-why-does-a-tissue-need-to-control-its-own-blood-flow","type":"data-interpretation","stem":"An arteriole narrows to half its original radius, with other determinants unchanged. Approximately how does its resistance change?","options":{"A":"It becomes eight times its original value overall.","B":"It becomes sixteen times its original value.","C":"It becomes twice its original value.","D":"It becomes four times its original value."},"answer":"B","why":"Vascular resistance varies inversely with the fourth power of radius; halving radius increases resistance by 2⁴ = 16.","not":{"A":"Eightfold change would follow a cubic, not fourth-power relation.","C":"Twofold change assumes a linear relation with radius.","D":"Fourfold change would follow a square, not fourth-power relation."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-2:62676c05","chapter":"local-regulation","section":"2-autoregulation-steady-flow-despite-changing-pressure","type":"mechanism","stem":"Perfusion pressure to a tissue falls moderately but remains in the autoregulatory range. What compensatory change is expected?","options":{"A":"Arteriolar constriction that limits the fall in flow.","B":"Arteriolar dilation that limits the fall in flow.","C":"Venous dilation that increases upstream resistance.","D":"Capillary contraction that restores the lost pressure."},"answer":"B","why":"When perfusion pressure falls, the arteriolar wall is stretched less (myogenic) and metabolites build up (metabolic). Both relax the arterioles, lowering resistance so flow stays close to normal despite the lower pressure.","not":{"A":"Constriction would worsen the reduction in flow.","C":"Venous dilation does not directly lower arteriolar resistance.","D":"Capillaries are not the contractile resistance vessels."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-2:d8d70736","chapter":"local-regulation","section":"2-autoregulation-steady-flow-despite-changing-pressure","type":"data-interpretation","stem":"A graph of organ flow against perfusion pressure is almost flat over a middle range but slopes downward at very low pressures. Why?","options":{"A":"The arterioles reach their maximal constriction at low pressure.","B":"The veins become the principal resistance at low pressure.","C":"The arterioles reach their maximal dilation at low pressure.","D":"The heart stops driving any pressure into the organ."},"answer":"C","why":"Once arterioles are fully dilated, they cannot reduce resistance further, so flow decreases as pressure falls.","not":{"A":"Maximal constriction occurs in response to rising pressure, not very low pressure.","B":"Venous vessels do not replace arterioles as the chief resistance site.","D":"A downward slope does not mean perfusion pressure is zero."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-2:24f79465","chapter":"local-regulation","section":"3-two-mechanisms-myogenic-and-metabolic","type":"mechanism","stem":"Which observation most strongly points to a myogenic rather than metabolic adjustment?","options":{"A":"Constriction follows a rise in vessel-wall stretch.","B":"Dilation follows accumulation of local lactate.","C":"Dilation follows reduced tissue oxygen supply.","D":"Constriction follows removal of excess local metabolites."},"answer":"A","why":"The myogenic response is the smooth muscle reacting to stretch itself: more stretch → more contraction. Responses driven by O₂, CO₂, H⁺, lactate or other chemicals are metabolic.","not":{"B":"Lactate accumulation is a metabolic signal for dilation.","C":"Low oxygen is a metabolic signal for dilation.","D":"Metabolite washout belongs to metabolic autoregulation."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-2:35fec543","chapter":"local-regulation","section":"3-two-mechanisms-myogenic-and-metabolic","type":"application","stem":"Arterial pressure inside an arteriole is unchanged, but pressure in its surrounding tissue rises. What happens to transmural pressure?","options":{"A":"It decreases because the pressure difference narrows.","B":"It increases because the pressure difference widens.","C":"It becomes identical to intravascular arterial pressure.","D":"It remains fixed because arterial pressure is unchanged."},"answer":"A","why":"Transmural pressure equals internal pressure minus surrounding tissue pressure; increasing the latter reduces the difference.","not":{"B":"A higher external pressure reduces, rather than widens, the difference.","C":"It equals internal pressure only when external pressure is zero.","D":"Transmural pressure depends on both internal and external pressure."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-2:a6217db8","chapter":"local-regulation","section":"3-two-mechanisms-myogenic-and-metabolic","type":"data-interpretation","stem":"Two cylindrical vessels have equal transmural pressure, but vessel X has twice the radius of vessel Y. How do their required wall tensions compare?","options":{"A":"Both vessels require exactly the same wall tension.","B":"Vessel X requires half the wall tension of Y.","C":"Vessel X requires four times the tension of Y.","D":"Vessel X requires twice the wall tension of Y."},"answer":"D","why":"For a cylinder, wall tension T = P × r. With equal transmural pressure, the vessel with twice the radius needs twice the tension. This is why a capillary’s tiny radius lets a one-cell wall withstand blood pressure.","not":{"A":"Equal pressure alone does not make tension equal.","B":"Half the tension would apply to half the radius.","C":"A fourfold difference does not follow the linear radius relation."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-2:3ad16f51","chapter":"local-regulation","section":"3-two-mechanisms-myogenic-and-metabolic","type":"mechanism","stem":"Blood supply to an actively metabolising region becomes insufficient. Which local chemical pattern favours dilation?","options":{"A":"Reduced oxygen with decreased potassium and lactate.","B":"Reduced oxygen with increased carbon dioxide and H⁺.","C":"Increased oxygen with reduced carbon dioxide and H⁺.","D":"Increased oxygen with increased pH and lower temperature."},"answer":"B","why":"Inadequate oxygen supply and accumulation of CO₂ and H⁺ signal a mismatch between demand and flow, causing dilation.","not":{"A":"Low potassium and lactate do not represent the expected buildup.","C":"This pattern signals good oxygen supply and fewer acidic metabolites.","D":"Higher pH and cooler tissue are not the stated metabolic dilator pattern."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-2:807ad2b5","chapter":"local-regulation","section":"3-two-mechanisms-myogenic-and-metabolic","type":"application","stem":"A region of heart muscle starts working harder. Which local product of its own metabolism helps increase its blood flow?","options":{"A":"Serotonin released by nearby platelets.","B":"Adenosine formed as ATP is used up.","C":"Noradrenaline from sympathetic nerves.","D":"Angiotensin II formed in the blood."},"answer":"B","why":"Working muscle uses ATP faster, and its breakdown products (adenosine, ADP, AMP) build up and dilate the local arterioles. Flow rises in proportion to how hard the tissue works, which is why the heart relies so heavily on this.","not":{"A":"Platelet serotonin constricts vessels, and it comes from platelets, not from the working muscle.","C":"Noradrenaline is a nerve transmitter that mainly constricts arterioles via α₁ receptors.","D":"Angiotensin II is a circulating constrictor, not a product of the muscle’s metabolism."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-2:e8e3b7dd","chapter":"local-regulation","section":"3-two-mechanisms-myogenic-and-metabolic","type":"mechanism","stem":"Once local metabolic dilation restores an organ’s blood flow, what prevents dilation from continuing unchecked?","options":{"A":"The restored flow abolishes vessel-wall smooth muscle.","B":"The restored flow removes oxygen from the tissue cells.","C":"The restored flow clears accumulated dilator metabolites.","D":"The restored flow causes a sustained rise in local lactate."},"answer":"C","why":"The dilators exist because flow was too low for demand. Once flow matches demand, they are washed away as fast as they are made, so the arterioles regain their tone. This negative feedback stops dilation running away.","not":{"A":"Vascular smooth muscle remains present and functional.","B":"Restored delivery generally improves tissue oxygen availability.","D":"Sustained lactate accumulation would favour continued dilation."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-2:d4cc948f","chapter":"local-regulation","section":"3-two-mechanisms-myogenic-and-metabolic","type":"application","stem":"An injured small vessel has activated platelets nearby. Which released substance would favour local constriction?","options":{"A":"Nitric oxide produced by the vascular endothelium.","B":"Histamine acting through vascular H₂ receptors.","C":"Bradykinin released through endothelial signalling.","D":"Serotonin released from the activated platelets."},"answer":"D","why":"Activated platelets release serotonin, which constricts the injured vessel and reduces bleeding while a clot forms.","not":{"A":"NO is a vasodilator.","B":"The H₂-mediated histamine response described is dilation.","C":"Bradykinin promotes dilation through endothelial NO."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-2:62c0b932","chapter":"local-regulation","section":"3-two-mechanisms-myogenic-and-metabolic","type":"application","stem":"A small cutaneous arteriole is exposed to cold. Which response is most consistent with the lecture?","options":{"A":"Immediate suppression of sympathetic vasoconstrictor tone.","B":"No change unless baroreceptor firing also rises.","C":"Local dilation that rapidly increases heat loss.","D":"Local constriction that helps reduce blood flow."},"answer":"D","why":"Cold makes skin arterioles constrict locally, cutting skin blood flow so less heat is lost from the body surface. Warmth has the opposite effect.","not":{"A":"Cold favours local smooth-muscle contraction rather than sympathetic withdrawal.","B":"Local control can occur without a baroreflex change.","C":"Warmth and metabolic activity, rather than cold, favour dilation here."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-2:d49d869f","chapter":"local-regulation","section":"4-active-and-reactive-hyperaemia","type":"application","stem":"An exercising leg receives increased blood flow while its muscle continues contracting. What is this response called?","options":{"A":"Active hyperaemia driven by ongoing tissue metabolism.","B":"Baroreflex vasodilation after systemic arterial pressure rises.","C":"Reactive hyperaemia following a temporary flow block.","D":"Autoregulation that holds its flow at rest level."},"answer":"A","why":"Active hyperaemia is the rise in flow while a tissue is working: its metabolites dilate the arterioles for as long as activity continues. Reactive hyperaemia is the surge after a blockage is released.","not":{"B":"The increase originates largely from local demand, not a pressure-rise baroreflex.","C":"Reactive hyperaemia is a surge after an obstruction ends.","D":"The flow rises above baseline rather than staying constant."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-2:142d8356","chapter":"local-regulation","section":"4-active-and-reactive-hyperaemia","type":"data-interpretation","stem":"Two identical limbs have arterial flow interrupted for one minute and five minutes respectively. Which reperfusion pattern is expected?","options":{"A":"Neither block produces an overshoot after release.","B":"The five-minute block produces a larger, longer surge.","C":"Both blocks produce identical flow responses in time.","D":"The one-minute block produces a larger and more lasting surge."},"answer":"B","why":"Longer interruption creates a greater oxygen debt and metabolite buildup, producing a larger and more prolonged reactive response.","not":{"A":"Reactive hyperaemia is expected when flow is restored.","C":"The amount of accumulated metabolites depends on occlusion duration.","D":"A shorter block generally produces a smaller oxygen debt."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-2:cd37ef54","chapter":"local-regulation","section":"5-signals-from-the-endothelium","type":"mechanism","stem":"An intact endothelium releases nitric oxide in response to blood flow. How does this relax neighbouring smooth muscle?","options":{"A":"It diffuses into the muscle and increases cGMP.","B":"It directly increases angiotensin II receptor activation.","C":"It raises platelet serotonin inside the vessel wall.","D":"It enters the muscle and decreases its cGMP."},"answer":"A","why":"NO is a gas, so it diffuses from the endothelium straight into the smooth muscle. There it activates guanylyl cyclase, raising cGMP, which lowers Ca²⁺ and relaxes the muscle.","not":{"B":"Angiotensin II signalling is a constrictor pathway.","C":"Serotonin favours constriction rather than NO-mediated relaxation.","D":"NO acts through an increase, not a decrease, in cGMP."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-2:8a0f203d","chapter":"local-regulation","section":"5-signals-from-the-endothelium","type":"mechanism","stem":"Bradykinin reaches an intact arteriole. Which pathway best explains the vasodilation described in the lecture?","options":{"A":"Platelet stimulation followed by serotonin release.","B":"Reduction of endothelial relaxing-factor production.","C":"Endothelial stimulation followed by nitric oxide release.","D":"Direct activation of smooth-muscle angiotensin II receptors."},"answer":"C","why":"Bradykinin acts on the endothelium, which responds by making nitric oxide. NO then relaxes the smooth muscle. If the endothelium is damaged, bradykinin dilates much less.","not":{"A":"Platelet serotonin favours constriction.","B":"Suppressing NO formation would reduce the dilator response.","D":"Angiotensin receptor activation favours constriction."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-2:1bf86b19","chapter":"local-regulation","section":"5-signals-from-the-endothelium","type":"application","stem":"Which of these locally released substances dilates arterioles?","options":{"A":"Histamine","B":"Serotonin","C":"Endothelin-1","D":"Angiotensin II"},"answer":"A","why":"Histamine, released from mast cells in injury and allergy, relaxes arteriolar smooth muscle (via H₂ receptors) and makes capillaries leakier, which is why inflamed tissue turns red and swollen.","not":{"B":"Serotonin from activated platelets constricts the injured vessel to limit bleeding.","C":"Endothelin-1 from the endothelium is one of the most powerful constrictors known.","D":"Angiotensin II constricts arterioles and raises resistance."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-2:ddfdcdbc","chapter":"local-regulation","section":"6-local-control-and-the-whole-body","type":"application","stem":"During exercise, sympathetic activity increases throughout the body, yet working muscle receives much more blood. Which explanation is best?","options":{"A":"A higher systemic pressure redirects flow equally among tissues.","B":"Systemic arteriolar constriction raises every tissue’s flow equally.","C":"Local metabolic dilation outweighs constriction in active muscle.","D":"Local metabolic constriction outweighs dilation in active muscle."},"answer":"C","why":"Active muscle generates enough vasodilator metabolites to oppose sympathetic constriction, redirecting blood to the tissue that needs it.","not":{"A":"Sympathetic activity often remains elevated; metabolites oppose its constrictor effect locally.","B":"Constriction does not raise flow equally across every organ.","D":"Metabolites in working muscle predominantly dilate its arterioles."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-2:aab1d43a","chapter":"neural-humoral","section":"1-why-does-the-body-need-central-control","type":"data-interpretation","stem":"Cardiac output falls by 20%, but total peripheral resistance rises by 25%. What happens to mean arterial pressure in the simplified MAP relation?","options":{"A":"It remains approximately unchanged.","B":"It decreases by approximately one quarter.","C":"It increases by approximately one quarter.","D":"It decreases by approximately one fifth."},"answer":"A","why":"In MAP ≈ CO × TPR, 0.80 × 1.25 = 1.00, so the changes cancel mathematically.","not":{"B":"The combined product does not fall by one quarter.","C":"That ignores the fall in cardiac output.","D":"That ignores the compensatory rise in resistance."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-2:de24dd96","chapter":"neural-humoral","section":"1-why-does-the-body-need-central-control","type":"data-interpretation","stem":"Heart rate rises from 70 to 80 beats/min while stroke volume stays at 70 mL. By how much does cardiac output increase?","options":{"A":"By 1.40 litres per minute.","B":"By 0.80 litres per minute.","C":"By about 0.49 litres per minute.","D":"By 0.70 litres per minute."},"answer":"D","why":"The increase is 10 beats/min × 70 mL/beat = 700 mL/min, or 0.70 L/min.","not":{"A":"1.40 L/min doubles the actual change.","B":"0.80 L/min treats the extra beats as a larger volume.","C":"0.49 L/min does not follow from the given rate change."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-2:b0c72fd6","chapter":"neural-humoral","section":"1-why-does-the-body-need-central-control","type":"application","stem":"A drug primarily dilates large systemic veins rather than arterioles. Which immediate circulatory change is most likely?","options":{"A":"More blood is stored in the venous reservoir.","B":"Peripheral resistance rises sharply in the arterioles.","C":"Capillary smooth-muscle contraction becomes stronger.","D":"More blood is forced towards the right atrium."},"answer":"A","why":"Venodilation increases capacitance, allowing veins to hold more blood and tending to reduce venous return.","not":{"B":"Arteriolar tone is the principal determinant of peripheral resistance.","C":"Capillaries are not the main contractile vessels.","D":"Venoconstriction, not venodilation, mobilises stored blood."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-2:39324bf1","chapter":"neural-humoral","section":"2-the-control-centre-in-the-medulla","type":"mechanism","stem":"Electrical stimulation of the medullary pressor area increases arterial pressure. What is the principal neural pathway?","options":{"A":"Excitation of sympathetic neurons in the spinal cord.","B":"Inhibition of sympathetic neurons in the spinal cord.","C":"Direct stimulation of the carotid sinus sensory fibres.","D":"Direct inhibition of firing in the sinoatrial pacemaker."},"answer":"A","why":"The pressor area sends excitatory fibres down to the sympathetic neurons in the spinal cord. More sympathetic output constricts arterioles and veins and speeds and strengthens the heart, so pressure rises.","not":{"B":"Inhibiting sympathetic neurons would tend to lower pressure.","C":"Carotid sinus fibres carry sensory information towards the medulla.","D":"Pressor-area activity does not require direct pacemaker inhibition."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-2:bad895da","chapter":"neural-humoral","section":"2-the-control-centre-in-the-medulla","type":"mechanism","stem":"Damage selectively removes the inhibitory effect of the medullary depressor area on the pressor area. Which response is expected?","options":{"A":"Reduced local metabolism with fixed arteriolar diameter.","B":"Greater sympathetic discharge with increased vascular tone.","C":"Greater vagal discharge with isolated venous dilation.","D":"Reduced sympathetic discharge with markedly decreased vascular tone."},"answer":"B","why":"The depressor area normally inhibits the pressor area; removing this brake permits more sympathetic vasoconstrictor activity.","not":{"A":"Local tissue metabolism is not directly abolished by this lesion.","C":"A selective loss of pressor inhibition does not primarily increase vagal output.","D":"Disinhibition would increase, not decrease, pressor drive."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-2:f41e9481","chapter":"neural-humoral","section":"2-the-control-centre-in-the-medulla","type":"recall","stem":"Where in the medulla do incoming baroreceptor signals first arrive?","options":{"A":"The cervical sympathetic ganglion.","B":"The sinoatrial pacemaker node.","C":"The nucleus tractus solitarius.","D":"The anterior pituitary gland."},"answer":"C","why":"Afferents in IX and X end in the nucleus tractus solitarius (NTS). The NTS is the sorting station: it excites the depressor area and the vagal neurons, which together set sympathetic and vagal output.","not":{"A":"A sympathetic ganglion transmits efferent autonomic signals.","B":"The SA node is an effector pacemaker, not the sensory relay.","D":"The anterior pituitary is not the stated first medullary relay."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-2:8ddfc026","chapter":"neural-humoral","section":"3-sympathetic-control-of-the-blood-vessels","type":"mechanism","stem":"Most sympathetic constrictor fibres release noradrenaline onto arteriolar smooth muscle. Which receptor mainly mediates constriction?","options":{"A":"The beta-2 adrenergic receptor on vessels.","B":"The muscarinic M2 receptor.","C":"The alpha-1 adrenergic receptor.","D":"The endothelial H2 receptor."},"answer":"C","why":"Noradrenaline binds α₁ receptors on arteriolar smooth muscle, raising intracellular Ca²⁺ and contracting the muscle. This raises resistance and arterial pressure.","not":{"A":"β₂ activation typically dilates muscle arterioles.","B":"M₂ receptors mediate vagal slowing of the heart.","D":"H₂ receptors respond to histamine, which dilates arterioles."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-2:6fa794cd","chapter":"neural-humoral","section":"3-sympathetic-control-of-the-blood-vessels","type":"mechanism","stem":"A sympathetic nerve supplying an arteriole is cut, and nothing else changes. Why does the arteriole usually widen?","options":{"A":"Its background dilator firing is increased.","B":"Its capillaries begin to generate pressure.","C":"Its muscle stops responding to metabolites.","D":"Its background constrictor firing is removed."},"answer":"D","why":"Sympathetic fibres to arterioles fire all the time (sympathetic tone), keeping the vessel partly constricted. Cutting the nerve removes that steady constriction, so the arteriole relaxes. This is also how the nerves dilate a vessel normally: by firing less, not by a separate dilator signal.","not":{"A":"Most arterioles have no tonic dilator nerve supply to increase.","B":"Capillaries have no smooth muscle and generate no driving pressure.","C":"Local metabolic control still works after the nerve is cut."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-2:c23d87fc","chapter":"neural-humoral","section":"3-sympathetic-control-of-the-blood-vessels","type":"application","stem":"A patient has a sudden rise in arteriolar sympathetic firing with minimal change in cardiac output. Which immediate variable rises most directly?","options":{"A":"Systemic venous compliance.","B":"The rate of tissue metabolite clearance.","C":"Total peripheral resistance.","D":"The intrinsic rate of the SA node."},"answer":"C","why":"Arteriolar constriction increases resistance and therefore tends to raise arterial pressure even at stable output.","not":{"A":"Sympathetic venoconstriction generally reduces venous compliance.","B":"Metabolite clearance depends on local blood flow, not this direct action.","D":"SA nodal rate concerns cardiac autonomic effects rather than arteriolar action."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-2:a35881be","chapter":"neural-humoral","section":"3-sympathetic-control-of-the-blood-vessels","type":"recall","stem":"Which transmitter–effect combination describes the special sympathetic vasodilator fibres discussed in the lecture?","options":{"A":"Acetylcholine with constriction in small capillary beds.","B":"Acetylcholine with dilation in skeletal muscle vessels.","C":"Noradrenaline with dilation in the great veins.","D":"Noradrenaline with dilation in most superficial skin vessels."},"answer":"B","why":"Some sympathetic fibres to skeletal muscle release acetylcholine and dilate its vessels, for example in anticipation of exercise. They are the exception to the rule that sympathetic nerves constrict; their importance in humans is debated.","not":{"A":"Capillaries do not contain the required smooth-muscle layer.","C":"Noradrenaline is chiefly a venoconstrictor rather than a dilator.","D":"Most cutaneous noradrenergic fibres are constrictors."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-2:53f9d0c7","chapter":"neural-humoral","section":"4-control-of-the-heart-sympathetic-and-vagus","type":"application","stem":"A recording shows that cardiac vagal stimulation lowers the firing rate of the sinoatrial node. Which additional effect is expected?","options":{"A":"Slower impulse conduction through the AV node.","B":"Faster impulse conduction through the AV node.","C":"Faster pacemaker discharge through alpha-1 activation.","D":"Stronger ventricular contraction through beta-1 activation."},"answer":"A","why":"Parasympathetic acetylcholine acts at muscarinic M₂ receptors to slow both SA nodal firing and AV nodal conduction.","not":{"B":"AV conduction is slowed rather than accelerated by vagal action.","C":"The major cardiac pacemaker effect is not mediated by α₁.","D":"β₁ activation describes sympathetic, not vagal, stimulation."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-2:58bb2ad3","chapter":"neural-humoral","section":"4-control-of-the-heart-sympathetic-and-vagus","type":"data-interpretation","stem":"An isolated SA node fires at about 100 beats/min, whereas the same person’s resting pulse was about 70. Which interpretation best fits?","options":{"A":"Atrial conduction had slowed the spontaneous SA nodal rate.","B":"The cardiac pacemaker was intrinsically limited to 70.","C":"Resting vagal activity had restrained SA nodal firing.","D":"Resting sympathetic activity had restrained SA nodal firing."},"answer":"C","why":"With no nerve input the SA node fires at about 100 beats/min. At rest the vagus releases acetylcholine continuously (vagal tone), holding the rate down to about 60–80. Blocking the vagus, for example with atropine, lets the rate rise.","not":{"A":"Atrial conduction is not the main cause of the slower intrinsic SA pacemaker rate.","B":"The isolated rate shows the pacemaker is capable of firing faster.","D":"Sympathetic activity usually increases SA nodal firing."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-2:b5267abe","chapter":"neural-humoral","section":"4-control-of-the-heart-sympathetic-and-vagus","type":"mechanism","stem":"Which pair of effects does sympathetic stimulation of the heart produce?","options":{"A":"Faster SA firing and stronger ventricular contraction.","B":"Slower SA firing and stronger ventricular muscle contraction.","C":"Slower SA firing and weaker ventricular contraction.","D":"Faster SA firing and slower AV nodal conduction."},"answer":"A","why":"Sympathetic noradrenaline acts on β₁ receptors in the heart: the SA node fires faster (higher rate), conduction through the AV node speeds up, and the ventricular muscle contracts more strongly (higher contractility).","not":{"B":"The combination reverses the normal effects on rate and contractility.","C":"Those changes are opposite to the typical sympathetic effects.","D":"Sympathetic stimulation generally speeds rather than slows AV conduction."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-2:c3efa58e","chapter":"neural-humoral","section":"5-hormones-that-act-on-the-circulation","type":"mechanism","stem":"During a stress response, circulating adrenaline dilates vessels in working skeletal muscle while constricting many skin vessels. Why?","options":{"A":"Skin tissue receives a much lower concentration of adrenaline.","B":"The muscle vessels lack any adrenergic receptors.","C":"Different vascular beds contain markedly different amounts of plasma.","D":"Different vascular beds express different adrenergic receptors."},"answer":"D","why":"β₂ effects can favour dilation in skeletal muscle, while α₁ effects cause constriction in many skin and gut vessels.","not":{"A":"The difference is due mainly to receptor distribution rather than selective circulating delivery.","B":"Muscle vessels respond because they possess adrenergic receptors.","C":"Plasma volume differences do not explain opposite receptor responses."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-2:08844f41","chapter":"neural-humoral","section":"5-hormones-that-act-on-the-circulation","type":"mechanism","stem":"A fall in renal perfusion activates renin release. Which resulting substance directly constricts arterioles and promotes aldosterone release?","options":{"A":"Circulating angiotensin II.","B":"Circulating bradykinin.","C":"Circulating vasoactive intestinal peptide.","D":"Circulating atrial natriuretic peptide."},"answer":"A","why":"Renin-dependent formation of angiotensin II raises vascular resistance and stimulates aldosterone, thirst and ADH.","not":{"B":"Bradykinin promotes endothelial NO-mediated dilation.","C":"VIP is listed as a vasodilator.","D":"ANP promotes dilation and salt excretion."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-2:35f5d317","chapter":"neural-humoral","section":"5-hormones-that-act-on-the-circulation","type":"application","stem":"A patient has low circulating volume. Which hormone chiefly increases renal water retention and can constrict vessels at high concentration?","options":{"A":"Atrial natriuretic peptide.","B":"Vasoactive intestinal peptide.","C":"Endothelial prostacyclin.","D":"Vasopressin, also called ADH."},"answer":"D","why":"ADH promotes renal water conservation and has a vasoconstrictor action when its level is high.","not":{"A":"ANP promotes sodium and water loss.","B":"VIP is a vasodilator rather than a water-retaining hormone.","C":"Prostacyclin dilates vessels and limits platelet aggregation."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-2:e6b8f690","chapter":"neural-humoral","section":"5-hormones-that-act-on-the-circulation","type":"application","stem":"After a period of low blood volume, aldosterone secretion increases. Which effect is most important over the following hours to days?","options":{"A":"Greater renal sodium loss with accompanying water loss.","B":"Immediate electrical acceleration of the intrinsic cardiac pacemaker.","C":"Greater renal sodium retention with accompanying water retention.","D":"Immediate widening of resistance vessels across the circulation."},"answer":"C","why":"Aldosterone increases renal Na⁺ retention, and water follows, helping restore circulating volume over time.","not":{"A":"Sodium and water loss are opposite to aldosterone’s action.","B":"Direct pacemaker acceleration is an autonomic effect, not its main role.","D":"Its principal effect is renal volume conservation, not universal vasodilation."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-2:0e1df047","chapter":"neural-humoral","section":"5-hormones-that-act-on-the-circulation","type":"recall","stem":"Which group contains only vasodilators identified in the systemic humoral-control material?","options":{"A":"Bradykinin, ADH and ANP","B":"VIP, angiotensin II and ANP","C":"Bradykinin, VIP and endothelin-1","D":"Bradykinin, VIP and ANP"},"answer":"D","why":"Bradykinin (via endothelial NO), VIP and ANP all relax arterioles. Each wrong option slips in one constrictor, so you need to know every member of the list, not just recognise one name.","not":{"A":"ADH (vasopressin) constricts vessels at high concentrations.","B":"Angiotensin II is a powerful constrictor.","C":"Endothelin-1 is a powerful constrictor."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-2:80df08f5","chapter":"reflexes","section":"1-what-is-a-cardiovascular-reflex","type":"mechanism","stem":"A stretch receptor detects a change in arterial pressure. What is the next step before the medulla can adjust heart and vessels?","options":{"A":"The receptor directly releases a circulating chemical signal.","B":"The heart first changes pressure without neural signalling.","C":"Afferent nerve fibres carry its signal to the CNS.","D":"Efferent nerve fibres carry its signal to the receptor."},"answer":"C","why":"The receptor communicates with the central integrating site through a sensory afferent pathway before autonomic output changes.","not":{"A":"Baroreceptors are neural sensors, not general endocrine secretors.","B":"A reflex requires sensory input before its corrective output.","D":"Efferent fibres carry commands away from the CNS."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-2:a240dfd5","chapter":"reflexes","section":"2-baroreceptors-the-body-s-pressure-sensors","type":"mechanism","stem":"An arterial pressure transducer and a carotid sinus baroreceptor show similar responses to pressure changes. What physical property does the biological receptor actually detect?","options":{"A":"Sodium content of its arterial plasma.","B":"Stretch of its arterial wall.","C":"Oxygen content of its arterial blood.","D":"Temperature of its arterial blood."},"answer":"B","why":"Baroreceptors are stretch-sensitive nerve endings; pressure alters their firing by distending the vessel wall.","not":{"A":"Plasma sodium is not the direct stimulus described.","C":"Oxygen is a chemoreceptor stimulus, not baroreceptor stretch.","D":"Temperature is not the principal stimulus for the sinus baroreceptor."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-2:4b65146c","chapter":"reflexes","section":"2-baroreceptors-the-body-s-pressure-sensors","type":"recall","stem":"A pressure signal originates in an aortic arch baroreceptor. Which nerve normally carries it towards the medulla?","options":{"A":"The glossopharyngeal nerve, cranial nerve IX.","B":"The vagus nerve, cranial nerve X.","C":"The accessory nerve, cranial nerve XI.","D":"The hypoglossal nerve, cranial nerve XII."},"answer":"B","why":"Aortic arch baroreceptors send their signals up the vagus nerve (X) to the NTS. Carotid sinus signals travel in the glossopharyngeal nerve (IX).","not":{"A":"IX carries the carotid sinus afferent pathway.","C":"XI is not the afferent route from the aortic arch.","D":"XII is not a baroreceptor afferent route."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-2:36d0f4d8","chapter":"reflexes","section":"3-the-baroreflex-step-by-step","type":"mechanism","stem":"Arterial pressure increases suddenly and baroreceptor firing rises. What changes first in central reflex control?","options":{"A":"NTS activation increases depressor and vagal signalling.","B":"NTS activation increases pressor and spinal sympathetic firing.","C":"Sensory traffic to the medulla decreases despite added stretch.","D":"The SA node starts generating impulses without modulation."},"answer":"A","why":"More stretch → faster baroreceptor firing → more input to the NTS. The NTS excites the depressor area (which inhibits the pressor area) and the vagal neurons, so sympathetic output falls and vagal output rises.","not":{"B":"The reflex suppresses rather than increases sympathetic pressor drive.","C":"Increased wall stretch increases, rather than decreases, afferent traffic.","D":"The heart remains under autonomic regulation during the reflex."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-2:d26af9e1","chapter":"reflexes","section":"3-the-baroreflex-step-by-step","type":"mechanism","stem":"A person stands suddenly, and arterial pressure at the carotid sinus briefly falls. What happens to baroreceptor firing?","options":{"A":"It fails to return to baseline after pressure recovers.","B":"It increases as arterial-wall stretch decreases.","C":"It decreases as arterial-wall stretch decreases.","D":"It remains constant because the sinus is in the neck."},"answer":"C","why":"Falling arterial pressure reduces carotid sinus stretch and therefore lowers its afferent firing rate.","not":{"A":"Firing can increase again as blood pressure recovers.","B":"Less stretch leads to less, not more, discharge.","D":"The receptor responds to local pressure changes even in the neck."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-2:2fa3ce60","chapter":"reflexes","section":"3-the-baroreflex-step-by-step","type":"application","stem":"A haemorrhage suddenly lowers blood pressure. Which changes help restore it through the arterial baroreflex?","options":{"A":"Increased sympathetic drive and reduced vagal drive.","B":"Reduced sympathetic drive and greater venous storage.","C":"Reduced sympathetic drive and increased vagal drive.","D":"Increased vagal drive and reduced venous return."},"answer":"A","why":"Lower afferent firing releases the pressor area and reduces vagal inhibition, raising heart rate, contractility and vascular tone.","not":{"B":"Sympathetic withdrawal and venous storage would further lower pressure.","C":"That combination is appropriate to a rise in pressure.","D":"Greater vagal drive would slow the heart, while low return worsens output."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-2:63b64e4f","chapter":"reflexes","section":"3-the-baroreflex-step-by-step","type":"mechanism","stem":"After a fall in arterial pressure, sympathetic activity increases to both arterioles and veins. Why is the venous response useful?","options":{"A":"It increases storage of blood away from the heart.","B":"It increases return of stored blood to the heart.","C":"It prevents ventricular filling until pressure recovers.","D":"It directly increases renal excretion of circulating water."},"answer":"B","why":"Venoconstriction reduces venous capacitance, moving stored blood towards the heart and supporting stroke volume.","not":{"A":"Increased storage would reduce venous return.","C":"More venous return tends to support rather than prevent filling.","D":"The rapid venous effect is mechanical, not renal water excretion."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-2:f1e8e6a8","chapter":"reflexes","section":"3-the-baroreflex-step-by-step","type":"application","stem":"A patient has had high arterial pressure for months. Why might baroreceptors fail to drive pressure back to the original baseline?","options":{"A":"Their signals stop entering the medulla during hypertension.","B":"Their afferent pathways turn into motor pathways over time.","C":"Their operating range resets around the sustained level.","D":"Their stretch sensitivity increases indefinitely without adaptation."},"answer":"C","why":"When pressure stays high for days, the baroreceptors reset and treat the new level as normal, so they defend it instead of correcting it. Long-term pressure is set mainly by the kidneys’ control of salt and water.","not":{"A":"Resetting does not require loss of every afferent signal.","B":"Sensory fibres do not become motor fibres.","D":"Unbounded sensitivity would strongly oppose the persistent rise."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-2:d8bb613a","chapter":"reflexes","section":"3-the-baroreflex-step-by-step","type":"application","stem":"A patient with major blood loss remains poorly perfused despite tachycardia and constricted peripheral vessels. Which interpretation is best?","options":{"A":"The raised resistance confirms that organ flow is adequate.","B":"The baroreflex has failed because the heart rate has become elevated.","C":"The baroreflex is active but cannot restore the missing volume.","D":"The tachycardia proves that venous return is now normal."},"answer":"C","why":"The reflex can support arterial pressure by raising rate and resistance, but persistent low volume may still limit cardiac output and organ perfusion.","not":{"A":"Pressure compensation cannot guarantee sufficient tissue flow.","B":"Tachycardia is an expected sign of a functioning low-pressure reflex.","D":"Heart rate alone does not measure venous return."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-2:a3a03e3a","chapter":"reflexes","section":"4-low-pressure-receptors-sensing-how-full-the-circulation-is","type":"recall","stem":"Which locations house the low-pressure receptors described in the lecture?","options":{"A":"The carotid sinus and adjacent stretch-sensitive aortic arch walls.","B":"The atria and adjacent pulmonary venous circulation.","C":"The cerebral cortex and adjacent pituitary gland.","D":"The kidneys and adjacent renal collecting ducts."},"answer":"B","why":"Low-pressure stretch receptors are described in atrial walls at venous entries and in the pulmonary circulation.","not":{"A":"Carotid and aortic receptors mainly sense arterial pressure.","C":"The cortex and pituitary are not the specified volume-receptor sites.","D":"Renal structures regulate volume but are not the named atrial receptors."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-2:482610cf","chapter":"reflexes","section":"4-low-pressure-receptors-sensing-how-full-the-circulation-is","type":"application","stem":"An atrial stretch receptor fires more during late atrial diastole when venous filling increases. Which receptor type fits best?","options":{"A":"Type B, reflecting greater atrial filling.","B":"An aortic body receptor, reflecting low oxygen.","C":"A carotid sinus receptor, reflecting arterial stretch.","D":"Type A, reflecting stronger atrial contraction."},"answer":"A","why":"The lecture identifies type B atrial receptors as filling-sensitive and more active when the atrial wall is distended.","not":{"B":"Aortic body receptors monitor blood chemistry.","C":"Carotid sinus receptors monitor high-pressure arterial stretch.","D":"Type A activity is linked to atrial systole."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-2:edd630f0","chapter":"reflexes","section":"4-low-pressure-receptors-sensing-how-full-the-circulation-is","type":"mechanism","stem":"A rapid infusion overfills the atria. Which renal hormonal response helps lower the excess circulating volume?","options":{"A":"Increased aldosterone release and increased sodium storage.","B":"Reduced ANP release and reduced sodium excretion.","C":"Reduced ADH release and increased water excretion.","D":"Increased ADH release and reduced water excretion."},"answer":"C","why":"Atrial stretch signalling reduces ADH drive, allowing more water to be excreted when circulating volume is excessive.","not":{"A":"Aldosterone-mediated sodium retention tends to expand volume.","B":"ANP is stimulated, not suppressed, by atrial stretch.","D":"High ADH retains water and would worsen overload."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-2:903cc9ed","chapter":"reflexes","section":"4-low-pressure-receptors-sensing-how-full-the-circulation-is","type":"mechanism","stem":"Venous return rises and stretches the walls of the atria. Which hormone does the heart then release to help shed the extra volume?","options":{"A":"Aldosterone, which acts on the kidney tubules.","B":"Vasopressin, which acts on the collecting ducts.","C":"Angiotensin II, which acts on the arterioles.","D":"ANP, which acts on the kidneys and the vessels."},"answer":"D","why":"Stretched atrial muscle releases atrial natriuretic peptide (ANP). It makes the kidneys excrete more sodium and water and relaxes vessels, so volume and pressure come down.","not":{"A":"Aldosterone comes from the adrenal cortex and makes the kidneys keep sodium.","B":"Vasopressin comes from the posterior pituitary and makes the kidneys keep water.","C":"Angiotensin II is formed in the blood and conserves salt and volume."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-2:c4d61d94","chapter":"reflexes","section":"4-low-pressure-receptors-sensing-how-full-the-circulation-is","type":"application","stem":"An isolated atrial stretch response raises heart rate even as vasodilation tends to lower pressure. Which interpretation best fits?","options":{"A":"A resting vagal response to decreased circulating volume.","B":"A Bainbridge-type reflex responding to excess venous return.","C":"A Cushing response to reduced blood supply to the medulla.","D":"An arterial baroreflex responding to increased arterial stretch."},"answer":"B","why":"The lecture’s atrial volume reflex can accelerate the heart to move excess venous blood onwards despite concomitant vasodilation.","not":{"A":"Reduced volume triggers arterial baroreflex tachycardia, not this overfilling response.","C":"Cushing hypertension and bradycardia result from raised intracranial pressure.","D":"An arterial pressure rise normally evokes bradycardia."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-2:7a88b269","chapter":"reflexes","section":"5-chemoreceptors","type":"mechanism","stem":"A patient has low arterial oxygen and high carbon dioxide. Which peripheral sensors respond mainly to these chemical changes?","options":{"A":"The atrial type A and type B volume receptors.","B":"The carotid sinus and aortic arch baroreceptors.","C":"The sinoatrial and atrioventricular pacemaker nodes.","D":"The carotid bodies and the aortic bodies."},"answer":"D","why":"Peripheral chemoreceptors in the carotid and aortic bodies monitor oxygen, carbon dioxide and hydrogen ions, chiefly stimulating breathing.","not":{"A":"Atrial volume receptors principally detect filling and stretch.","B":"Arterial baroreceptors principally detect stretch.","C":"Cardiac nodes generate and conduct impulses rather than monitor arterial chemistry."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-2:d0e8d761","chapter":"reflexes","section":"5-chemoreceptors","type":"mechanism","stem":"Blood flow to the medullary vasomotor centre becomes severely reduced. Which local chemical change directly activates its sympathetic response?","options":{"A":"Hyperoxia together with falling carbon dioxide.","B":"Reduced hydrogen ions together with abundant oxygen.","C":"Hypoxia together with accumulating carbon dioxide.","D":"Increased oxygen delivery together with reduced acidity."},"answer":"C","why":"Poor medullary perfusion produces hypoxia and hypercapnia, which strongly stimulate the vasomotor centre to raise pressure.","not":{"A":"High oxygen and low CO₂ do not represent medullary ischaemia.","B":"Low acidity and abundant oxygen oppose the described stimulus.","D":"Adequate oxygen delivery would not produce the ischaemic response."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-2:cb1aa724","chapter":"reflexes","section":"6-the-cushing-reflex-when-the-brain-is-squeezed","type":"application","stem":"A patient develops rapidly increasing intracranial pressure after a brain haemorrhage. What first threatens cerebral perfusion?","options":{"A":"Immediate loss of blood volume from the systemic arteries.","B":"Increased cerebral perfusion despite rising intracranial pressure.","C":"Compression of brain vessels and reduced driving pressure.","D":"Compensatory dilation of brain vessels with increased driving pressure."},"answer":"C","why":"Rising intracranial pressure compresses intracranial vessels and lowers cerebral perfusion pressure, risking medullary ischaemia.","not":{"A":"Raised intracranial pressure does not require systemic blood loss.","B":"Perfusion initially becomes harder as intracranial pressure rises.","D":"Dilation cannot prevent the loss of cerebral driving pressure as intracranial pressure rises."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-2:bb196f12","chapter":"reflexes","section":"6-the-cushing-reflex-when-the-brain-is-squeezed","type":"mechanism","stem":"Why may arterial blood pressure rise sharply during severe medullary ischaemia caused by raised intracranial pressure?","options":{"A":"Direct vasomotor-centre excitation greatly increases sympathetic outflow.","B":"Increased vagal discharge is the main cause of the pressure rise.","C":"Direct vasomotor-centre inhibition greatly decreases total sympathetic outflow.","D":"Increased atrial ANP release is the main cause of the pressure rise."},"answer":"A","why":"Hypoxic, hypercapnic medullary tissue drives a strong sympathetic pressor response to restore cerebral blood flow.","not":{"B":"Vagal discharge slows the heart and is not the primary pressor mechanism.","C":"Inhibition would tend to lower vascular tone and pressure.","D":"ANP favours dilation and volume loss."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-2:c6451892","chapter":"reflexes","section":"6-the-cushing-reflex-when-the-brain-is-squeezed","type":"mechanism","stem":"A patient with raised intracranial pressure develops hypertension and bradycardia together. What best explains the slow pulse?","options":{"A":"The cardiac pacemaker stops responding to vagal signals.","B":"The hypertension directly activates cardiac beta-1 receptors.","C":"The atrial volume reflex reacts to the sympathetic pressure rise.","D":"The arterial baroreflex reacts to the sympathetic pressure rise."},"answer":"D","why":"Medullary ischaemia first raises sympathetic pressure; arterial baroreceptors then increase vagal drive and slow the heart.","not":{"A":"Cardiac autonomic signalling remains central to the response.","B":"β₁ stimulation would usually accelerate, not slow, the heart.","C":"Atrial volume receptors respond chiefly to filling, not this sequence."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-2:eeb60098","chapter":"reflexes","section":"6-the-cushing-reflex-when-the-brain-is-squeezed","type":"application","stem":"After severe head injury, a patient has high blood pressure, a slow pulse and irregular breathing. What does this combination suggest?","options":{"A":"An uncomplicated rise in exercise-related cardiac output and flow.","B":"A dangerous Cushing response to intracranial compression.","C":"A typical active-hyperaemia response in skeletal muscle.","D":"An isolated atrial volume reflex after rapid infusion."},"answer":"B","why":"Hypertension, reflex bradycardia and abnormal respiration form the Cushing triad, a late sign of severe intracranial pressure.","not":{"A":"Exercise more commonly raises both rate and output.","C":"Local muscle dilation does not explain the full neurological triad.","D":"Atrial overfilling usually causes tachycardia in the lecture’s account."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-2:87092d3f","chapter":"reflexes","section":"7-other-inputs-and-the-lecture-s-exceptions","type":"application","stem":"A person becomes angry during an argument, and both pulse and pressure rise. Which route can initiate this response?","options":{"A":"Atrial filling signals acting through the kidneys on increased urine output.","B":"Platelet serotonin acting through capillaries on cardiac pacing.","C":"Cerebral CSF signals acting through the ventricles on the heart.","D":"Cortical signals acting through the hypothalamus on autonomic centres."},"answer":"D","why":"Emotions such as anger begin in the cortex. The cortex drives the hypothalamus, which excites the vasomotor centre, raising sympathetic output so heart rate and blood pressure rise.","not":{"A":"Renal volume control is too slow and not the primary emotional route.","B":"Platelet serotonin is a local vascular constrictor, not the main emotional signal.","C":"The CSF pathways do not directly account for this autonomic response."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-2:eaee88fc","chapter":"reflexes","section":"7-other-inputs-and-the-lecture-s-exceptions","type":"application","stem":"A brief sharp pain raises a patient’s pressure, but prolonged severe pain later causes faintness. What best accounts for the difference?","options":{"A":"Early pressor activation gives way to dilation and hypotension.","B":"Early venous pooling gives way to strong vasoconstriction.","C":"Long pain increases total peripheral resistance without limit.","D":"Brief pain reduces sympathetic output from central pathways."},"answer":"A","why":"The lecture distinguishes the pressor response to short, sharp pain from depressor vasodilation and possible syncope with sustained pain.","not":{"B":"The directions of vascular changes are reversed in this option.","C":"Prolonged pain may produce hypotension rather than unbounded resistance.","D":"Short sharp pain can stimulate rather than inhibit pressor activity."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-2:48774172","chapter":"reflexes","section":"7-other-inputs-and-the-lecture-s-exceptions","type":"application","stem":"A student faints after receiving distressing news, with a slow pulse and low arterial pressure. Which mechanism best matches the lecture?","options":{"A":"Severe medullary ischaemia causing a Cushing pressor response.","B":"Emotional inhibition of pressor activity with vagal predominance.","C":"Metabolic stimulation of the heart’s sympathetic beta-1 receptors.","D":"Atrial volume-receptor firing after excessive fluid infusion."},"answer":"B","why":"Fear or grief can favour a depressor, vagal response with bradycardia, vasodilation and hypotension.","not":{"A":"Cushing physiology produces hypertension with reflex bradycardia.","C":"β₁ stimulation would generally raise the pulse and contractility.","D":"Overfilled atria may produce a faster pulse in the described reflex."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-2:d631685a","chapter":"reflexes","section":"7-other-inputs-and-the-lecture-s-exceptions","type":"mechanism","stem":"Inflation of the lungs sends sensory impulses towards the medulla. What cardiovascular response is described in the lecture?","options":{"A":"Atrial volume-receptor excitation with sustained vasoconstriction.","B":"Carotid sinus chemoreceptor inhibition with increased arterial tone.","C":"Sympathetic afferent excitation of vasomotor activity with constriction.","D":"Vagal afferent inhibition of vasomotor activity with vasodilation."},"answer":"D","why":"The lecture states that lung-inflation afferents travel in the vagus and can inhibit the vasomotor centre, lowering vascular tone and pressure.","not":{"A":"This is a lung-inflation input rather than an atrial filling response.","B":"The carotid sinus is a stretch receptor, and the described effect is dilation.","C":"The named afferent pathway is vagal and its effect is depressor."}}]};
